        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.743,
            0.065,
            0.835,
            0.082
          ],
          "content": "Symmetry"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.121,
            0.326,
            0.142
          ],
          "content": "Here is one way:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.333,
            0.135,
            0.615,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.364,
            0.834,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "We have seen figures having 4 and 2 angles of symmetry. Can we get a figure having exactly 3 angles of symmetry? Can you use radial arms for this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.433,
            0.833,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": "Let us try with 3 radial arms as in the figure below. How many angles of symmetry does it have and what are they?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.478,
            0.563,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "Here is a figure with three radial arms."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.508,
            0.623,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.712,
            0.833,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": "Trace and cut out a copy of this figure. By rotating the cutout over this figure determine its angles of rotation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.757,
            0.834,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "We see that only a full turn or a rotation of 360° will bring the figure back into itself. So this figure does not have rotational symmetry as 360 degrees is its only angle of symmetry."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.826,
            0.833,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "However, can anything in the figure be changed to make it have 3 angles of symmetry?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.884,
            0.941,
            0.917,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
